WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.740
As a dark reimagining of Superman's origin
story, Brightburn doesn't disappoint.

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:09.240
From the requisite Superman references to
nods to producer James Gunn's other properties,

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:12.660
here are some small details from Brightburn
that you might have missed.

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:13.780
Spoilers ahead.

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:18.480
Right from the start, the plot elements stolen
straight from Superman are obvious.

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:21.240
An alien baby crash lands on a farm in Kansas.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.041
Later, he can fly and has super strength and
red eyes and heat vision.

00:00:24.041 --> 00:00:28.280
He even wears the blanket he was found in
as a cape like some versions of the Man of

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:28.780
Steel.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:33.000
But if there's a single visual most associated
with Superman, it would almost undoubtedly

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:37.250
be the cover to Action Comics #1: the first
appearance of the Man of Steel.

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:41.510
Before the superhero genre was crystallized
as a concept, here was a man in a garish red

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:46.330
and blue suit lifting a car over his head
as terrified onlookers fled away from him.

00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:49.240
Brightburn pays tribute to the visual in a
surprisingly subtle way.

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:53.660
After his uncle Noah finds out about his violent
tendencies, Brandon begins tormenting the

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:55.100
guy on a dark stretch of road.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:57.400
"Nope, no, nooooo."

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:00.420
Finally, Brandon lifts the car up into the
air off-camera.

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:04.520
The filmmakers don't make it overly explicit,
but it's undeniably a reference to Action

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:08.630
Comics although in the source material, the
occupant in the car presumably didn't smash

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:10.500
his face in half on the steering wheel.

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:15.860
There's one more major iconic moment from
Superman history that Brightburn subverts:

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:16.920
Superman saving a plane.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:20.940
It's an endlessly repeated moment for the
Man of Steel that appears again and again

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:23.549
in nearly every visual adaptation he's had
to date.

00:01:23.549 --> 00:01:27.609
There's good reason for that; it shows exactly
the scale of problems that Superman is willing

00:01:27.609 --> 00:01:28.500
and able to handle.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:31.460
A plane hurtling towards the ground is a job
for Superman.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.220
Brightburn prefers to turn this symbolism
on its head.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:45.920
The end of the film sees Brandon drop his
adopted mother to the ground from high in

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:46.440
the sky.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:51.200
Freed from the last remnants of his humanity,
he turns to see an airplane hurtling through

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:52.060
the air towards him.

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:56.240
The scene cuts to a demolished airplane that's
destroyed the rest of the Breyer household,

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:58.040
leaving Brandon the only obvious survivor.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.480
But enough about Brightburn's relationship
to Superman.

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:05.679
The film pulled inspiration from plenty of
other famous pop culture works, too.

00:02:05.679 --> 00:02:09.319
For starters, the symbol that Brandon draws
throughout the film looks a lot like symbols

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:10.380
used in other works.

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:14.280
Most clearly, it resembles a symbol used in
both Berserk and Nameless.

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:18.640
The former is a famous manga that's known
for gruesome violence, otherworldly evil,

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:22.060
and a demonic presence summoned by a symbol
called the Brand of Sacrifice.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:24.620
It's a close match for the symbol that Brandon
draws.

00:02:24.629 --> 00:02:28.650
The symbol also makes an appearance in Chris
Burnham and Grant Morrison's comic book Nameless,

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.820
where it represents the insanity of an other-dimensional
god.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:35.700
In fact, the symbol in Nameless is nearly
a carbon copy of Brandon's symbol.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:39.920
Regardless of where the filmmakers draw their
inspiration, it's clearly meant to signify

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:42.040
otherworldly evil and meaningless violence.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:46.280
Even if you caught all the above references,
you still might have missed some of the small

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:48.340
details relating directly to the film.

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:51.060
For starters, did you notice Brandon's wardrobe
changing?

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:55.280
Early on, his spaceship pulses with red light
which seems to activate his more homicidal

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:55.780
urges.

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:59.700
After that, his wardrobe gains more splashes
of red the more evil he becomes.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:03.870
What starts with a red hood on a grey sweatshirt
eventually leads to an almost entirely red

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:04.720
soccer shirt.

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:08.500
He quite literally goes from wearing shades
of grey as someone who can still be redeemed

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:10.959
to matching the blood he spills in the color
he wears.

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:14.390
It's arguably most obvious in the scene when
he attacks Erica in the diner.

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:18.260
He sends a shard of glass into one of her
eyes, and when she pulls it out, she can only

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:19.520
see through a red tint.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:23.420
Brandon stays either on the red side of her
vision or right in the middle for nearly the

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:28.409
entire scene, showcasing his transition from
nice alien kid to murdery alien kid.

00:03:29.409 --> 00:03:33.219
While we're on the subject, Erica's suffering
doesn't end with the attack in the diner.

00:03:33.219 --> 00:03:37.140
As the audience, and Brandon's mother Tori,
find out later in the film, the police can't

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:38.720
find Erica's body for a reason.

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:43.200
Brandon takes her back to the barn in order
to perform a grotesque dissection, presumably

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.040
to figure out what makes people work.

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:49.240
"Here, pass it along."

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:53.379
It's actually a moment in the film that's
set up fairly early on in a subtle way.

00:03:53.379 --> 00:03:57.860
On Brandon's dresser, he has an anatomical
frog statue that's been partially opened up.

00:03:57.860 --> 00:04:01.620
It sort of implies that the alien sociopath
has been thinking about what's inside people

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:04.840
and animals for a lot longer than the movie
explicitly suggests.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:10.780
Even though James Gunn didn't direct Brightburn,
his influence as a producer is clearly felt.

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:14.580
Even beyond the use of violence and horror
movie trappings, a familiar face makes an

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:15.660
appearance in the film.

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:20.720
As the credits roll, frequent James Gunn collaborator
Michael Rooker appears as a YouTube personality

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:25.220
named Big T, screaming about how the "fake
news" is keeping "Brightburn" a secret from

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:26.340
the general populace.

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:30.560
Even though it's just a small cameo for the
Guardians of the Galaxy star, Gunn and Rooker

00:04:30.569 --> 00:04:32.410
have collaborated on tons of projects together.

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:36.180
At this point, we wouldn't be surprised if
the two are contractually obligated to work

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:36.680
together.

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:42.080
Rooker's cameo isn't the only connective tissue
that Brightburn has to other James Gunn movies.

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:46.630
During the Big T's video sermon, he brings
up other monsters and urban legends that might

00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:50.949
be "working with" Brightburn to destroy America,
including an aquatic creature and a woman

00:04:50.949 --> 00:04:52.300
who strangles men with rope.

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:55.140
Are those evil Brightburn-style Aquaman and
Wonder Woman?

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:55.740
Maybe!

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:59.920
But the real Easter egg here is that one of
those pictures show Rainn Wilson's Crimson

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:01.900
Avenger from Gunn's earlier film, Super.

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:03.640
"Shut up, crime."

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:08.560
In addition, the diner that Brandon attacks
midway through Brightburn is called Darbo's.

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:12.540
In Super, the Crimson Avenger's real name
is Frank Darbo, and he works as a short-order

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:13.040
cook.

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:15.100
Sounds like Frank's really moving up in the
world!

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:18.180
"He's got a diner, he's got a diner in Kansas.

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:20.720
So who knows how he got it, but yes he does."

